    "created_at": "2026-08-13T23:00:02Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-08-13T23:02:26Z",
    "body": "https://github.com/search?q=%2F%28%3F-i%29%5B%5E%5Cs%23%5D%5B%5Ct+%5D*%23%5Cs*%5Ba-z%5D%2B%3A+*%5Ba-zA-Z%5D%2F+language%3Apython&type=code\nStill a fair bit of false-positives. Examples include:\n- `# default: foo`\n- `# returns: foo` \n- `# units: m/s`\n- `# example: foo`\n- `# note: foo`\n\nWhile these comments still apply directly to the line they're on, they're for humans and not for tooling, so it doesn't matter if the line is split or not.\n\n\nAlso worth highlighting is `# pragma: no-cover`. I'm not super familiar with Coverage.py, but my understanding is that these must be on the same line as the colon opening the block (or on the following line, but that's irrelevant here). So either of these work:\n```py\nif True:  # pragma: no-cover\nif (\n    True\n):  # pragma: no-cover\n```\nIf they are captured by our heuristic, they will be unnecessarily prevented from splitting, and we don't want to special-case certain tools.\n\n\nP.S., if we do adopt a heuristic such like this, we can stop explicitly checking for `pylint` comments, but `noqa` needs to stay special-cased, since it can omit the `:` section, as well as often being uppercased. We could also probably stop explicitly checking `type` comments, but it might make sense to still, since they can be part of the AST, and we already special-case them to reformat them.",
    "author_association": "COLLABORATOR",
